# Closed Subschemes: Unions and Intersections

## Setup and definitions

A **closed subscheme** of a scheme $X$ is an equivalence class of closed immersions $Z \hookrightarrow X$, or equivalently (and concretely) a closed subset $Y \subseteq X$ together with a structure sheaf $\mathcal{O}_Z$ fitting into a short exact sequence
$$0 \to \mathcal{I} \to \mathcal{O}_X \to i_*\mathcal{O}_Z \to 0$$
where $i: Z \hookrightarrow X$ is the inclusion and $\mathcal{I}$ is a **quasi-coherent ideal sheaf** of $\mathcal{O}_X$. Conversely, every quasi-coherent ideal sheaf $\mathcal{I} \subseteq \mathcal{O}_X$ determines a unique closed subscheme $Z = V(\mathcal{I})$ with structure sheaf $\mathcal{O}_X/\mathcal{I}$. This gives a bijection
$$\{\text{closed subschemes of } X\} \;\longleftrightarrow\; \{\text{quasi-coherent ideal sheaves } \mathcal{I} \subseteq \mathcal{O}_X\}.$$

## Finite union of closed subschemes

Let $Z_1, \dots, Z_n$ be closed subschemes of $X$, corresponding to quasi-coherent ideal sheaves $\mathcal{I}_1, \dots, \mathcal{I}_n \subseteq \mathcal{O}_X$.

**Definition.** The **union** $Z_1 \cup \cdots \cup Z_n$ is the closed subscheme of $X$ defined by the ideal sheaf
$$\mathcal{I}_{1} \cap \cdots \cap \mathcal{I}_{n}.$$

**Justification.** The underlying topological space of the closed subscheme $V(\mathcal{J})$ is $\{x \in X : \mathcal{J}_x \subseteq \mathfrak{m}_x\}$, i.e. the set of points where $\mathcal{J}$ is contained in the maximal ideal of the local ring. We have
$$V(\mathcal{I}_1 \cap \cdots \cap \mathcal{I}_n) = V(\mathcal{I}_1) \cup \cdots \cup V(\mathcal{I}_n),$$
since a prime (maximal) ideal contains $\bigcap \mathcal{I}_i$ if and only if it contains some $\mathcal{I}_i$. (For prime $\mathfrak{p}$: $\bigcap \mathcal{I}_i \subseteq \mathfrak{p} \Rightarrow \mathcal{I}_i \subseteq \mathfrak{p}$ for some $i$, because if every $\mathcal{I}_i \not\subseteq \mathfrak{p}$, pick $a_i \in \mathcal{I}_i \setminus \mathfrak{p}$; then $\prod a_i \in \bigcap \mathcal{I}_i$ but $\prod a_i \notin \mathfrak{p}$, contradiction.) The reverse inclusion is immediate. Hence the underlying set is the union of the underlying sets, which is the correct topological behavior. The scheme structure is the one induced by $\bigcap \mathcal{I}_i$.

**Why finite.** The intersection $\mathcal{I}_1 \cap \cdots \cap \mathcal{I}_n$ of finitely many quasi-coherent ideal sheaves is again quasi-coherent (it is the kernel of the map $\mathcal{O}_X \to \bigoplus \mathcal{O}_X/\mathcal{I}_i$, a map of quasi-coherent sheaves, and kernels of morphisms of quasi-coherent sheaves are quasi-coherent). For an **arbitrary** (possibly infinite) family, the intersection of quasi-coherent subsheaves need not be quasi-coherent in general, so the construction does not extend to infinite unions of closed subschemes within the category of schemes.

## Arbitrary intersection of closed subschemes

Let $\{Z_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$ be an arbitrary (possibly infinite) family of closed subschemes of $X$, corresponding to quasi-coherent ideal sheaves $\{\mathcal{I}_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$.

**Definition.** The **intersection** $\bigcap_{\alpha} Z_\alpha$ is the closed subscheme of $X$ defined by the ideal sheaf
$$\mathcal{I} = \sum_{\alpha \in A} \mathcal{I}_\alpha \;\subseteq\; \mathcal{O}_X,$$
i.e. the sheaf generated by all the $\mathcal{I}_\alpha$ (the sheaf-theoretic sum, which is the image of $\bigoplus_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha \to \mathcal{O}_X$).

**Justification.** The sum $\sum_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha$ is quasi-coherent: it is the image of a morphism of quasi-coherent sheaves (the direct sum $\bigoplus \mathcal{I}_\alpha$ is quasi-coherent, being a direct sum of quasi-coherent sheaves — this holds for arbitrary direct sums on a scheme), and images of morphisms of quasi-coherent sheaves are quasi-coherent. Hence $\sum_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha$ defines a closed subscheme.

On underlying topological spaces:
$$V\!\left(\sum_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha\right) = \bigcap_\alpha V(\mathcal{I}_\alpha),$$
since a prime/maximal ideal contains $\sum \mathcal{I}_\alpha$ if and only if it contains every $\mathcal{I}_\alpha$. This is the correct set-theoretic intersection, and it works for **arbitrary** families because the sum of quasi-coherent sheaves is always quasi-coherent (unlike the intersection, which may fail to be).

**Summary of the duality.** The reversal of operations is the hallmark of the Galois-type correspondence between ideals and their vanishing sets:

| Operation on closed subschemes | Operation on ideal sheaves |
|---|---|
| Finite union $Z_1 \cup \cdots \cup Z_n$ | Intersection $\mathcal{I}_1 \cap \cdots \cap \mathcal{I}_n$ |
| Arbitrary intersection $\bigcap_\alpha Z_\alpha$ | Sum $\sum_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha$ |

The asymmetry (finite vs. arbitrary) comes entirely from the quasi-coherence requirement: finite intersections and arbitrary sums of quasi-coherent sheaves are quasi-coherent, but arbitrary intersections need not be.

## The affine case

Now suppose $X = \operatorname{Spec} R$ is affine. The global section functor gives an equivalence of categories between quasi-coherent sheaves on $X$ and $R$-modules, and under this equivalence, quasi-coherent ideal sheaves $\mathcal{I} \subseteq \mathcal{O}_X$ correspond bijectively to **ideals** $I \subseteq R$. Explicitly, $\mathcal{I} = \widetilde{I}$, the tilde construction, and $\Gamma(X, \widetilde{I}) = I$. A closed subscheme $Z \subseteq X$ with ideal sheaf $\widetilde{I}$ is the affine scheme $\operatorname{Spec}(R/I)$ with the natural closed immersion $\operatorname{Spec}(R/I) \to \operatorname{Spec}(R)$.

### Finite union in the affine case

Let $Z_i = V(I_i) = \operatorname{Spec}(R/I_i)$ for ideals $I_1, \dots, I_n \subseteq R$. The union is:
$$Z_1 \cup \cdots \cup Z_n = V(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n) = \operatorname{Spec}\!\left(R/(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n)\right).$$

**Explicit description.** The ideal sheaf of the union is $\widetilde{I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n}$, and since the tilde construction commutes with finite intersections of submodules (kernels of maps between quasi-coherent sheaves correspond to kernels of the corresponding module maps), we have
$$\widetilde{I_1} \cap \cdots \cap \widetilde{I_n} = \widetilde{I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n}.$$
The structure sheaf of the union is $\mathcal{O}_X / \widetilde{I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n} = \widetilde{R/(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n)}$.

**Remark (Chinese remainder).** When the ideals $I_1, \dots, I_n$ are pairwise comaximal ($I_i + I_j = R$ for $i \neq j$), the Chinese Remainder Theorem gives
$$R/(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n) \cong R/I_1 \times \cdots \times R/I_n,$$
so the union is a disjoint union (coproduct) of the $Z_i$ as schemes. In general, $R/(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n)$ retains information about how the $Z_i$ overlap scheme-theoretically.

### Arbitrary intersection in the affine case

Let $Z_\alpha = V(I_\alpha)$ for an arbitrary family of ideals $\{I_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A}$ in $R$. The intersection is:
$$\bigcap_\alpha Z_\alpha = V\!\left(\sum_\alpha I_\alpha\right) = \operatorname{Spec}\!\left(R \big/ \sum_\alpha I_\alpha\right).$$

**Explicit description.** The ideal $\sum_\alpha I_\alpha$ is the ideal of $R$ generated by all the $I_\alpha$, i.e. the set of all *finite* sums $\sum_{\text{finite}} a_{\alpha}$ with $a_\alpha \in I_\alpha$ (only finitely many nonzero terms, since $R$ is a ring and ideals are closed under finite sums). The ideal sheaf of the intersection is
$$\widetilde{\sum_\alpha I_\alpha} = \sum_\alpha \widetilde{I_\alpha},$$
where the equality holds because the tilde construction commutes with arbitrary sums of submodules (the sum is the image of $\bigoplus_\alpha \widetilde{I_\alpha} \to \widetilde{R} = \mathcal{O}_X$, and tilde commutes with arbitrary direct sums and with images). The structure sheaf is $\widetilde{R/\sum_\alpha I_\alpha}$.

**Special case: two closed subschemes.** For $Z_1 = V(I)$, $Z_2 = V(J)$:
- Union: $Z_1 \cup Z_2 = V(I \cap J) = \operatorname{Spec}(R/(I\cap J))$.
- Intersection: $Z_1 \cap Z_2 = V(I + J) = \operatorname{Spec}(R/(I+J))$.

## Verification of the key sheaf-module correspondences

We confirm the two commutativity facts used above, which are the heart of the affine description:

1. **Tilde commutes with finite intersections of submodules.** For submodules $M_1, M_2 \subseteq M$ with $M$ finitely presented (in particular for ideals $I_1, I_2 \subseteq R$), we have $\widetilde{M_1 \cap M_2} = \widetilde{M_1} \cap \widetilde{M_2}$. This follows from the left-exactness of the tilde functor applied to the exact sequence $0 \to M_1 \cap M_2 \to M_1 \oplus M_2 \to M_1 + M_2 \to 0$ (the map being $(a,b) \mapsto a - b$), using that tilde is exact on finitely presented modules. For ideals of $R$ (which are submodules of the finitely presented module $R$), this applies. Finite intersections follow by induction.

2. **Tilde commutes with arbitrary sums of submodules.** For any family of submodules $M_\alpha \subseteq M$, the sum $\sum M_\alpha$ is the image of $\bigoplus M_\alpha \to M$. The tilde functor commutes with arbitrary direct sums ($\widetilde{\bigoplus M_\alpha} = \bigoplus \widetilde{M_\alpha}$, since on a distinguished open $D(f)$, sections are $(\bigoplus M_\alpha)_f = \bigoplus (M_\alpha)_f$ as $R_f$-modules) and with cokernels (always, for any module map), hence with images. Therefore $\widetilde{\sum_\alpha M_\alpha} = \sum_\alpha \widetilde{M_\alpha}$. Applied to ideals $I_\alpha \subseteq R$: $\widetilde{\sum I_\alpha} = \sum \widetilde{I_\alpha}$.

These two facts are precisely what make the affine description work, and they also explain the finite/arbitrary asymmetry: fact (1) requires finite presentation hypotheses (hence finiteness), while fact (2) holds without restriction (hence arbitrary families are allowed).

## Conclusion

- A **finite union** of closed subschemes $Z_1, \dots, Z_n$ of $X$ (with ideal sheaves $\mathcal{I}_1, \dots, \mathcal{I}_n$) is the closed subscheme defined by $\mathcal{I}_1 \cap \cdots \cap \mathcal{I}_n$. Finiteness is required because finite intersections of quasi-coherent ideal sheaves are quasi-coherent, while arbitrary intersections need not be.

- An **arbitrary intersection** of closed subschemes $\{Z_\alpha\}$ (with ideal sheaves $\{\mathcal{I}_\alpha\}$) is the closed subscheme defined by $\sum_\alpha \mathcal{I}_\alpha$. Arbitrariness is permitted because arbitrary sums of quasi-coherent sheaves are quasi-coherent.

- When $X = \operatorname{Spec} R$ is affine and $Z_i = V(I_i)$, these become:
  - **Union:** $Z_1 \cup \cdots \cup Z_n = V(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n) = \operatorname{Spec}\bigl(R/(I_1 \cap \cdots \cap I_n)\bigr)$.
  - **Intersection:** $\bigcap_\alpha Z_\alpha = V\bigl(\sum_\alpha I_\alpha\bigr) = \operatorname{Spec}\bigl(R/\sum_\alpha I_\alpha\bigr)$.

The operations on ideals are reversed relative to the operations on subschemes, reflecting the order-reversing nature of the correspondence $I \mapsto V(I)$.

